This sample needed a tiktoken install, which is deliberately NOT a dependency of this
repository -- so it cannot be regenerated by scripts/regen_samples.sh and is listed as
unreproducible there. The reproducible counterpart, which is what CI sees, is
samples/measure_request_construction.tokeniser-unavailable.out: same capture, and the token
columns say NOT MEASURED rather than estimating.

PRODUCED BY, recorded because a review pointed out that this file was the only evidence for
the token figures and said nothing about where they came from:

  uv run --with tiktoken python instruments/measure_request_construction.py \
      samples/fixtures/request_construction.dsh.jsonl \
      samples/fixtures/request_construction.hermes.jsonl \
      --tokeniser tiktoken:cl100k_base

Read BOTH bytes/token lines per arm. Over the whole body the two engines look ~30% apart --
but three quarters of the dsh body is provider metadata and none of the hermes body is, so
that gap is mostly the metadata's own density. Over MODEL INPUT, which is what a bill is
computed from, they are within ~3%. Quoting the first pair in a cost argument is the error
this instrument now prints both to prevent.

--- verbatim ---
CONFOUNDS -- printed every run, because each of these makes this number true about the RUN and false about the ENGINE:
  * A capture needs the client pointed at a NON-FIRST-PARTY base URL, and for Claude Code that is not a neutral act: it DISABLES tool-search deferral, which the source study measured at +49.5% tokens. A proxied Claude Code arm is a different configuration from a first-party one, not the same one observed.
  * Some clients embed LIVE GIT STATE in their own system prompt -- Hermes embeds cwd, branch, status and recent commit SUBJECTS. Such a capture is NOT reproducible across commits: committing changes the payload. Freeze one git state for a batch, or capture outside a repository, and say which you did.
  * A field PRESENT in a payload is not necessarily billed as model input. Check the vendor's own documentation per field before counting it; see PROVIDER_METADATA in this file for the two that are documented at zero.
  * The model never answers, so nothing that needs real model progress is measurable here: no compaction, no context growth across turns, no tool-use loop. This is the FIRST request, which is the fixed cost -- not a whole session.

tokeniser: tiktoken:cl100k_base

requests: 5 captured, 3 measurable, 2 startup probes (a probe is not a turn)
100% field coverage verified on all 3 measurable requests

arm / first request        wire B     body B   fixed B   convo B    meta B   tools   msgs
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
dsh                          8225       8680      1665       278      6519       3      1
hermes                      48820      49655     49426        84         0      20      1
  fixed B = system + tools, the part paid on EVERY turn; convo B = everything else in `messages`.

FIRST REQUEST -- arm 'dsh', endpoint /v1/messages, model named 'deepseek-flash'
    dsh_session_log              4172 B  <-- provider metadata: ZERO model-input tokens per vendor
    dsh_plugin_packages          2347 B  <-- provider metadata: ZERO model-input tokens per vendor
    tools                        1617 B
    messages                      278 B
    system                         48 B
    thinking                       19 B
    output_config                  18 B  <-- engine-specific, no counterpart in the comparators
    model                          16 B
    max_tokens                      6 B
    stream                          4 B
    (json framing)                155 B  (braces, quoted keys, separators)
    TOTAL                        8680 B  = the whole body, with nothing unaccounted
    3 tool schema(s) exposed, 1 message(s) in the conversation
    wire 8225 B vs normalised body 8680 B (ratio 0.948). The split above is of the NORMALISED serialization:
      a per-field share of the raw wire bytes is not well defined without re-parsing, and the two differ by the
      client's own separator and escaping choices. Quote `wire B` for what the network paid.
    provider metadata 6519 B (75.1% of the body): HTTP bytes, and ZERO model-input tokens per the vendor
    MODEL INPUT 2161 B -- the quantity a per-token bill is computed from
    tokens: body 2680, model input 526, provider metadata 2154
      3.24 bytes/token over the whole body, 4.11 bytes/token over MODEL INPUT.
      The second is the one a cost argument needs; the first includes provider metadata, which is billed at zero tokens and tokenises
      at its own density. Comparing whole-body densities across engines with different metadata compares the metadata, not the engines.

FIRST REQUEST -- arm 'hermes', endpoint /v1/chat/completions, model named 'capture-proxy-stub'
    tools                       37439 B
    messages                    12071 B
    stream_options                 23 B
    model                          20 B
    reasoning_effort                8 B
    stream                          4 B
    (json framing)                 90 B  (braces, quoted keys, separators)
    TOTAL                       49655 B  = the whole body, with nothing unaccounted
    20 tool schema(s) exposed, 1 message(s) in the conversation
    wire 48820 B vs normalised body 49655 B (ratio 0.983). The split above is of the NORMALISED serialization:
      a per-field share of the raw wire bytes is not well defined without re-parsing, and the two differ by the
      client's own separator and escaping choices. Quote `wire B` for what the network paid.
    NOTE: model input 49655 B and fixed 49426 B EXCEED wire 48820 B (101.7%). Not an error: this client serialises more
      compactly than json.dumps' default separators, so the normalised split is larger than what was sent. `wire B` is the observation; the rest is a re-serialisation.
    provider metadata 0 B: this client sends none, so model input and whole body are the SAME number here
    MODEL INPUT 49655 B -- the quantity a per-token bill is computed from
    tokens: body 11737, model input 11737, provider metadata 0
      4.23 bytes/token over the whole body, 4.23 bytes/token over MODEL INPUT.
      The second is the one a cost argument needs; the first includes provider metadata, which is billed at zero tokens and tokenises
      at its own density. Comparing whole-body densities across engines with different metadata compares the metadata, not the engines.

PER MODEL NAMED IN THE REQUEST ITSELF -- never one assumed model for the whole capture:
  capture-proxy-stub           2 request(s)  model input     51061 B  provider metadata        0 B
  deepseek-flash               1 request(s)  model input      2161 B  provider metadata     6519 B
exit=0
